**Key observations:**
- p is prime, so by Fermat's little theorem, a^p ≡ a (mod p), and a^(p-1) ≡ 1 (mod p)
- a^n mod p has period (p-1)
- n mod p cycles with period p
- So n·a^n mod p has period lcm(p-1, p) = p*(p-1)

**Approach:**
For each residue class of n modulo p*(p-1), check if n·a^n ≡ b (mod p). For each valid residue r in [1, p*(p-1)], count how many n in [1, x] have n ≡ r (mod p*(p-1)).

